# 2. Dynamic Active-Work Authority

The current module, tab, Agent, provider, workflow, slice, risk tier,
engineering status, Human-QA status, implementation authorization, protected
files, acceptance criteria and transition condition are defined only in:

`02 — TitanGold Current Active Work`

This Core Rule is stable.

The Current Active Work Rule is dynamic and should remain short.

It must define only:

- Current module or product area
- Current user-facing outcome
- Current slice or program slice
- Risk tier
- Authorized scope
- Hard boundaries
- Acceptance criteria
- Human-QA milestone
- Protected unrelated files
- Stop conditions
- Next approved transition

It must not repeat the entire Core Rule.

When the Current Active Work Rule is:

- Missing
- Empty
- Stale
- Contradictory
- Ambiguous
- Not explicitly authorizing implementation

then:

- Implementation authorization is NOT GRANTED
- Only safe read-only discovery is allowed
- Cursor must report the missing or conflicting scope
- Cursor must not guess the next task
- Cursor must not code, commit, deploy or mutate runtime state

A task-specific instruction may further narrow the Current Active Work Rule.

A task-specific instruction may grant implementation only for the exact
approved outcome.

It may not weaken this Core Rule.

---

# 3. Outcome-Driven Delivery Model

TitanGold work must be organized around a meaningful user or system outcome,
not around individual files, buttons, strings or tiny defects.

A valid work package should answer:

> What useful, testable result will exist for the user or system when this
> package is complete?

Examples of good outcomes:

- A user can securely configure, verify and manage a MEXC Connection
- An Agent produces truthful results with real history and safe controls
- Wallet can display deposit networks and safely prepare a withdrawal
- Scheduler executes approved analytical Agents under Emergency Stop
- A tab is fully useful in English and Persian across desktop and mobile

Examples of poor work-package boundaries:

- Change one button color
- Add one translation key
- Rename one label
- Add one API field
- Create one route without its consumer
- Create a form without persistence
- Create a backend service without a usable workflow

Small fixes may still be implemented, but they should normally be absorbed
inside the active outcome instead of becoming independent slices.

---

# 4. Work-Package Types

Every task must be classified as one of the following.

## 4.1 Read-Only Discovery

Purpose:

- Inspect architecture
- Reproduce defects
- Map dependencies
- Verify Sources of Truth
- Produce an implementation plan

No mutation is allowed.

## 4.2 Outcome Vertical Slice

The default implementation unit.

It completes one useful workflow across all affected layers.

## 4.3 Program Slice

Used for a large end-to-end product capability that cannot be made useful
through artificial micro-slices.

A Program Slice may include internal phases such as:

- Discovery
- Foundation
- Backend contract
- Provider integration
- Frontend workflow
- Capability mapping
- Security hardening
- Browser QA
- Human QA
- Closeout

Internal phases do not automatically require separate user approval,
separate Dynamic Rules or separate Human-QA cycles.

The Program Slice must still have one primary outcome, one Source-of-Truth
model and explicit high-risk gates.

Examples:

- MEXC End-to-End Connection Platform
- Wallet Deposit/Withdrawal/Transfer Workflow
- Complete Arbitrage Agent
- Futures Trading Foundation

## 4.4 Shared Foundation Work Package

Used only for a proven cross-cutting defect or a new shared capability needed
by multiple consumers.

## 4.5 Remediation Bundle

Used when Human QA or Browser QA finds multiple related low- or medium-risk
defects inside the same approved outcome.

Related defects should be fixed together when they share the same workflow or
acceptance criteria.

Do not create a new micro-slice for every button, label or spacing defect.

## 4.6 Documentation-Only Closeout

Used only after implementation and Human QA are complete.

It must not pretend that runtime code changed.

---

# 5. Risk-Tier Model

Every active work package must declare one risk tier.

## Tier 0 — Read-Only / Documentation

Examples:

- Discovery
- Audit
- Documentation correction
- Evidence verification

Requirements:

- Repository safety
- No mutation
- Accurate reporting
- No deployment unless a runtime artifact actually changed

## Tier 1 — Low Risk

Examples:

- Copy
- i18n
- RTL presentation
- Spacing
- Design-token alignment
- Empty-state text
- Non-security button styling
- Layout and responsiveness

Requirements:

- Scoped RCA
- Focused tests
- Browser QA
- Build/deploy when runtime UI changes
- Human QA at the parent outcome milestone, not for every minor fix

Cursor may fix additional reproduced Tier-1 defects within the same approved
surface without stopping for a new authorization.

## Tier 2 — Medium Risk

Examples:

- Frontend state
- API contract changes
- Safe read-only routes
- Status mapping
- Settings persistence
- Capability display
- Non-financial provider metadata
- Agent result contracts

Requirements:

- Full dependency and Source-of-Truth review
- Relevant backend/frontend tests
- Browser QA
- Deployment verification
- Human QA at the outcome milestone

Cursor may continue through related Tier-1 and Tier-2 defects when they remain
inside the approved acceptance criteria.

## Tier 3 — High Risk

Examples:

- Authentication
- Authorization
- Secrets
- Encryption
- Database migration
- Redis authority
- Scheduler
- Worker
- Runtime mode
- Kill Switch
- Provider private authentication
- Account permission discovery
- Financial status persistence

Requirements:

- Full RCA
- Full dependency audit
- Security review
- Deterministic tests
- Runtime provenance
- Explicit stop gates
- Explicit Human QA
- Separate closeout evidence

## Tier 4 — Critical Financial or External Side Effects

Examples:

- Real order placement
- Order cancellation or modification
- Real transfer
- Real withdrawal
- Settlement
- Account mutation
- P2P order placement
- Real notification delivery with operational consequences
- Enabling Live execution

Requirements:

- Explicit task-level authorization
- Explicit credential and environment authorization
- Operation-specific capability
- Idempotency
- Risk rules
- Limits
- Confirmation
- Audit
- Rollback or recovery plan
- Controlled test account or non-production environment where possible
- No irreversible automated test
- Human approval immediately before controlled real execution when required

Tier 4 work must never be authorized implicitly by a broad Program Slice.

