# 6. Continue-vs-Stop Rule

Cursor should continue within the current approved outcome when:

- A defect is Tier 1 or Tier 2
- It is reproduced
- It affects the same workflow
- It does not expand the Source of Truth
- It does not require a new migration
- It does not touch a closed baseline unexpectedly
- It does not introduce real external side effects
- It is covered by the existing acceptance criteria

Cursor must stop and request or report explicit approval when:

- Scope expands to a new user outcome
- A new database migration is required
- A new secret or credential owner is proposed
- A shared foundation defect is found
- A closed baseline must change
- Runtime safety semantics must change
- A real provider credential is required
- A real private provider request is required and not already authorized
- Any Tier-4 operation is required
- Repository state is uncertain
- Runtime provenance is uncertain
- Requirements conflict
- The implementation cannot remain fail-closed

The purpose of this rule is to preserve safety without stopping after every
minor defect.

