# 30. Git and Commit Strategy

Use a clean isolated worktree for scoped implementation when the original tree
contains protected work.

Implementation may use multiple meaningful commits when needed.

Good commit boundaries:

- Backend/provider contract
- Frontend workflow
- Tests
- Documentation closeout

Do not create commits only to record:

- Latest HEAD
- Implementation pointer
- Stamp
- Self-referential hash
- Documentation pointer chain

Do not rewrite pushed history merely to remove a harmless past pointer commit.

Do not create new ones.

Before closeout:

- Correct branch
- Clean scoped worktree
- Protected original work untouched
- Scoped commits
- No secrets
- Push complete
- Local committed HEAD equals remote HEAD
- Runtime source equals implementation baseline
- Served artifact equals implementation baseline
- Documentation HEAD recorded separately
- Rollback documented

