# 54. Artemis Core Completion Program — 15-Stage Roadmap

The Artemis program must be executed as a deliberate sequence of verifiable
capabilities. This roadmap prevents the central intelligence layer from becoming
an unbounded rewrite, a collection of disconnected experiments, or a premature
Live-trading shortcut.

This section defines **Artemis Core Completion**. Core completion means the
internal TitanGold evidence, orchestration, control, lineage, evaluation and
safe-maturity architecture is complete and verified for the approved maturity
stage. It does **not** mean that all future cognitive expansion, external-AI
advisory capabilities or Live automation are automatically enabled.

The Dynamic Active-Work Rule remains authoritative for which stage is currently
authorized. A later stage must not be inferred from the existence of this
roadmap.

The fifteen stages are:

## Stage 1 — Canonical Evidence Discovery

- Inventory every active Agent and its real outputs.
- Map stable Agent identity, DB record identity and legacy aliases.
- Define role and authority boundaries.
- Classify confidence, freshness, data quality and limitations truthfully.
- Design the canonical Agent-to-Artemis evidence contract from real system
  capabilities rather than from imagined fields.
- No implementation unless separately authorized.

## Stage 2 — Canonical Evidence Foundation

- Implement one versioned evidence envelope and validator.
- Implement canonical Agent identity normalization.
- Establish compatibility, nullability and unavailable semantics.
- Establish evidence lineage identifiers and contract version ownership.
- Preserve `ai_decisions` or another existing Source of Truth unless a separately
  approved persistence change is required.
- No Agent-specific parallel evidence schemas.

## Stage 3 — Agent-by-Agent Contract Compatibility

- Assess each Agent independently.
- Prefer adapters and targeted corrections over rewrites.
- Classify each Agent as contract-ready, adapter-ready, correction-required,
  implementation-required, non-evidence role or unavailable/deferred.
- Preserve the specialized user-facing Agent product while adding a truthful
  machine-consumable role.
- Do not force Risk, Portfolio, Optimization, Liquidity or Order Management into
  a false directional-vote model.

## Stage 4 — Evidence Validation, Persistence and Read-Only Ingestion

- Validate schema and identity.
- Persist or reconstruct evidence according to the approved Source-of-Truth
  design.
- Reject incompatible, stale or invalid evidence when policy requires.
- Distinguish unavailable from neutral.
- Preserve provenance, correlation family, limitations and confidence method.
- Artemis remains advisory and non-executing.

## Stage 5 — Artemis Orchestration Discovery

- Map the real current orchestrator, legacy mocks and remaining synthetic paths.
- Define conflict-resolution semantics.
- Define correlation-aware evidence grouping.
- Define missing-evidence behavior.
- Define role-aware authority handling.
- Define the minimum canonical orchestration boundary before implementation.

## Stage 6 — Real Artemis Evidence Orchestration

- Replace or contain synthetic Agent calls in the approved scope.
- Consume canonical evidence packages.
- Preserve every contributing and excluded Agent result.
- Resolve incompatible timeframes and horizons explicitly.
- Avoid naive majority voting and correlated double counting.
- Produce explainable analytical recommendations only.

## Stage 7 — Canonical Control Chain Integration

The required chain remains:

Artemis analytical decision
→ Risk veto and limits
→ Portfolio and Optimization bounded allocation/sizing
→ Liquidity execution-feasibility validation
→ Runtime mode, capability and Emergency Stop validation
→ Order Management
→ Provider only when explicitly authorized
→ Reconciliation and audit

At this stage:

- Risk owns veto authority.
- Portfolio and Optimization own only bounded sizing/allocation authority.
- Liquidity owns execution-feasibility authority.
- Order Management owns execution mechanics only.
- No component may silently absorb another component's authority.

## Stage 8 — Shadow Decision Mode

- Artemis makes time-stamped decisions against real market context without
  sending orders.
- Contributing evidence, exclusions, conflicts and control outcomes are recorded.
- Decisions are compared with future observed outcomes.
- No simulated success may be presented as realized performance.
- Shadow mode must remain operationally isolated from order placement.

## Stage 9 — Decision Lineage, Replay and Evaluation Foundation

- Every decision becomes reconstructable.
- Evidence, policy, configuration, model and implementation versions are linked.
- Replay distinguishes original historical evidence from current recomputation.
- Backtesting prevents look-ahead and future-data leakage.
- Evaluation is segmented by Agent role, market, symbol, timeframe, horizon,
  regime and version where meaningful.

## Stage 10 — Calibration and Promotion Framework

- Confidence calibration is measured when predictive claims are made.
- Trust or weighting policies become evidence-based and versioned.
- Promotion gates are explicit.
- A weak global average must not hide poor performance in a specific regime.
- Regression or degraded data quality can demote a component immediately.

## Stage 11 — Paper / Simulated Full-Chain Readiness

- Full decision → control → execution-intent chain runs with simulated orders.
- Risk, sizing, liquidity, idempotency, failure handling and reconciliation are
  exercised.
- No real funds are used.
- Paper execution must use the same contracts and gates intended for later
  controlled execution where practical.

## Stage 12 — Operational Reliability and Failure Recovery

Before any controlled real-effect environment:

- Provider failure behavior is verified.
- Worker and scheduler failure behavior is verified.
- Emergency Stop and runtime acknowledgement are verified.
- Duplicate execution is prevented.
- Reconciliation and incident recovery are tested.
- Runtime provenance is exact and auditable.
- Alerting and rollback procedures are documented.

## Stage 13 — Controlled Test-Environment Readiness

- Use only an explicitly approved environment.
- Use minimum credentials and capabilities.
- Bound assets, markets, operations, size and rate.
- Keep Tier-4 authorization separate from technical readiness.
- Human approval is required where real external side effects exist.

## Stage 14 — Limited Live Readiness Gate

A system may be technically ready for Limited Live only when Section 53 gates
are satisfied and the Dynamic Rule explicitly authorizes the operation.

Technical readiness never equals Live authorization.

No implementation, PR, Human-QA PASS, green CI result, confidence score or
Artemis recommendation may implicitly activate Live mode.

## Stage 15 — Artemis Core Completion and Freeze

Artemis Core may be declared complete only when:

- Canonical evidence is stable.
- Role-aware orchestration is real and explainable.
- Risk, Portfolio, Liquidity, runtime safety and Order Management boundaries are
  enforced.
- Decision lineage and evaluation are operational.
- Shadow and Paper evidence support the declared maturity.
- Remaining limitations are explicit.
- Human QA passes the approved milestone.
- Exact-main runtime provenance is verified.
- The completed baseline is closed and frozen.

After Stage 15, additional cognitive expansion, external-AI advisory systems,
provider infrastructure and self-improvement remain separate Program Slices.

Do not use “Artemis Core Complete” to imply:

- Every Agent product is complete.
- External AI providers are configured.
- The Multi-AI Council exists.
- Artemis is self-sufficient.
- Live trading is enabled.
- Expanded Live automation is authorized.

Section 53 remains the authoritative automated-trading maturity and Live gate.

