# 109. Production Build / Artifact Activation Safety

This section is non-negotiable. It refines Section 29 and Section 108 for
frontend artifacts. It does not authorize a production build, activation,
nginx reload or C2_052 execution by itself.

The canonical principle is:

> A production frontend artifact may be created only from an isolated clean
> named commit, written first to a non-live staging directory, verified,
> backed up against, and activated as a separate explicit operation.

An authorized `npm run build` is not permission to overwrite the live nginx
root from a dirty production-serving worktree.

## 109.1 Forbidden build origins

1. Building the frontend directly from a production-serving worktree is
   forbidden.
2. Building when Vite `outDir` (or equivalent) resolves to the current nginx
   live root, or writes inside that live root, is forbidden.
3. A dirty source tree may never be used to create a production / release
   artifact.
4. Untracked application source consumed by the frontend dependency graph is
   forbidden for release builds.

## 109.2 Required production artifact origin

Production artifacts must originate from all of:

- an isolated clean Git worktree
- a named commit SHA
- a locked dependency manifest (`package-lock.json` or the canonical lockfile)

Production build output must first be generated into a **non-live**
staging/release directory. It must not be written into the currently served
nginx dist during the build phase.

## 109.3 Activation is a separate operation

The currently served dist must have a verified backup **before** activation.

Activation must be a separate explicit operation after artifact verification.

Rollback must exist **before** activation. If a verified backup cannot be
created, activation must not proceed.

Activation targets only the live dist. It must not mutate application source,
backend processes, PM2, PostgreSQL or Redis.

## 109.4 Artifact provenance

Every production frontend artifact must record at least:

- source commit
- branch
- clean-tree proof
- package-lock SHA
- artifact tree SHA
- build timestamp / run ID

## 109.5 Hard-fail at the build command

`npm run build` or equivalent run against a live production root must **hard
fail** before Vite writes dist.

The guard must be wired into the normal frontend build lifecycle so it cannot
be silently skipped by the default `npm run build` path.

The guard must not rely only on an environment variable that anyone can casually
unset.

No instruction from ChatGPT, Cursor or an operator may bypass these gates
merely because a build itself was authorized.

A bypass, if ever architecturally required, needs a distinct Owner emergency
authorization artifact and must not be the default path.

## 109.6 Supported production flow

```text
CLEAN COMMIT
  → ISOLATED CLEAN WORKTREE
  → BUILD INTO NON-LIVE RELEASE DIRECTORY
  → ARTIFACT MANIFEST
  → VERIFY
  → BACKUP CURRENT LIVE DIST
  → EXPLICIT ACTIVATE
  → HTTPS SMOKE
  → ROLLBACK AVAILABLE
```

The legacy workflow “`npm run build` from `/home/ubuntu/webapp/TitanGold`
then nginx reload” is a known incident class and is refused by code.

## 109.7 Current live production identity

The current live production source root is:

`/home/ubuntu/webapp/TitanGold`

The current live nginx dist root is:

`/home/ubuntu/webapp/TitanGold/dist`

These identities are declared in-repo (`deploy/production-roots.json`) and may
additionally be marked on the server by `.titangold-production-root`. Creating
that marker on production is a separate Owner-gated activation step. Absence
of the marker does not authorize a live-root build when the configured path
matches.

## 109.8 Developer-local builds

Normal local/dev builds outside a declared production root, writing to a
non-live `outDir`, remain allowed. Release/production mode remains fail-closed.

